        <h2 id="thrust-ratio" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Thrust Ratio Explained
        </h2>

        <p>
          If you learn one specification in this category, make it this one. <strong>Thrust ratio</strong> describes the mechanical advantage between your hand and the plunger — a 10:1 gun delivers ten pounds of push at the cartridge for every pound you squeeze at the trigger. An 18:1 gun delivers eighteen.
        </p>

        <p>
          The number matters because sealants vary enormously in stiffness. A warm tube of acrylic latex flows under almost no pressure at all, and a cheap gun handles it perfectly well. A cold tube of polyurethane, or any construction adhesive, is a completely different proposition. On a 10:1 gun those materials require a grip most people cannot sustain for more than a few feet, and the bead suffers accordingly — you end up pumping the trigger, and every pump shows up in the joint as a ripple.
        </p>

        <p>
          Temperature compounds this. Sealant viscosity climbs sharply as it cools, so a tube that flowed easily at 70°F can feel like a different product at 40°F. Anyone caulking outdoors in spring or autumn is working with stiffer material than the tube was designed around, and a higher thrust ratio is what absorbs that.
        </p>

        <p>
          The counter-intuitive part is that more thrust is not universally better. High mechanical advantage means less feedback through the trigger, so on soft material a very high-ratio gun can over-deliver before you notice. Somewhere between 12:1 and 18:1 is the sweet spot for mixed household work, and anything below 10:1 is worth avoiding entirely.
        </p>

        <h2 id="dripless" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. What "Dripless" Really Means
        </h2>

        <p>
          The word is used loosely, and there are actually three different behaviours sold under it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Manual release</strong> is the baseline. A ratchet gun holds pressure until you press the thumb lever, and the tube keeps flowing until you do. This is not dripless at all, and it is why so many beads end in a blob — the sealant continues to emerge for a second or two after you stop moving.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Automatic pressure release</strong> is what a smooth-rod gun does. Let go of the trigger and the friction plate disengages, dropping the pressure immediately. This eliminates most of the problem for most materials and is the minimum worth buying.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>True dripless retraction</strong> goes further: releasing the trigger actively pulls the plunger back a fraction, which relieves the pressure inside the cartridge rather than merely stopping the push. On stiff sealant, which stays compressed for a surprisingly long time, this is the only mechanism that genuinely stops the flow dead.
        </p>

        <p>
          For occasional work the middle option is enough. If you are running long beads in polyurethane, or working overhead where a drip lands on you rather than the floor, the retracting mechanism is worth seeking out specifically.
        </p>


